  "narrative": "I registered a new round of context-specific refinements of the five largest supported dataset-specific associations, plus one mechanistically plausible new MDM2 probe. The central rationale is that the accepted effects are very large, so the most informative next question is not whether tiny additional main-effect probes are near zero, but whether the effects persist when co-altered genomic contexts and technical screen contexts differ. The proposed co-alteration main-effect tests examine SMARCA4 loss in ARID1A-loss and TP53-loss backgrounds, KEAP1 loss in TP53-loss background, PTEN loss in TP53-loss background, and TP53 loss in PTEN-wild-type background. These are complementary to prior interaction and co-restriction tests: interaction estimates are often imprecise and centered near zero, whereas direct conditional main effects tell whether the accepted contrast remains large inside a specific co-alteration stratum. The screen_roc_auc low/median robustness tests are not intended as causal claims about screen quality; they ask whether the associations are confined to apparently better screens. The CRISPR-Ky library tests ask whether the major associations are visible in a non-Avana technical setting. The new MYC-amplification/MDM2 test is restricted to TP53-wild-type cells because the MYC-MDM2-p53 axis is mechanistically most plausible there: MYC activation may create stronger MDM2 dependency when p53 signaling remains intact. This is a dataset-specific association hypothesis only, not a claim of causality or generalizability. These proposals are prioritized over near-null broad exploration because they either can establish important specificity of already strong effects or test a biologically focused new hypothesis with a clear p53-context rationale.",
  "research_notes": "Round 5 explore update. Accepted dataset-specific large associations after Round 4: H5 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency (validated); H17/H7-reversed KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency (validated); H14 PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency (validated); H8 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency (validated); H12 TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency (large and robust in subset but not independently validated yet). Current focus in Round 5 is specificity and robustness of those large effects rather than further near-null broad probes. Registered: co-alteration-restricted main effects for SMARCA4/SMARCA2 among ARID1A-loss and TP53-loss, KEAP1/NFE2L2 among TP53-loss, PTEN/STAT3 among TP53-loss, TP53/USP7 among PTEN-wt; low/median screen_roc_auc robustness tests for SMARCA4/SMARCA2, KEAP1/NFE2L2, PTEN/STAT3, MSI/WRN; Ky-library robustness tests for SMARCA4/SMARCA2 and TP53/USP7; new TP53-wt-restricted MYC amplification/MDM2 hypothesis. Active older probes remain but are lower priority than robustness of accepted large effects. Validation priority remains H12 TP53 loss/USP7 once enough supporting context exists. All conclusions remain associations in supplied lung cell-line dataset, not causal mechanisms or external generalization."
